A €20,000 ERP project can be a sensible investment. A €20,000 ERP project can also be the start of an expensive recovery. The difference is rarely the software license alone. When owners ask **how much does ERP cost**, the honest answer is that they are really asking what it will take to create reliable processes, trusted data, and a system people will actually use.

For a small or midsize business, a realistic ERP budget must cover more than the first invoice. It should account for implementation work, migration, integrations, internal time, training, and support after go-live. Buying too little scope creates workarounds. Buying too much creates complexity before the business needs it. The goal is not the biggest system. It is the right operational foundation.

## How Much Does ERP Cost? Start With the Full Picture

ERP costs generally fall into two categories: recurring software and infrastructure costs, and one-time project costs. The balance depends on your chosen platform, the number of users, the modules you need, and how close your current processes are to standard ERP workflows.

For an SME, annual software subscriptions may range from a few thousand dollars to significantly more for larger teams or specialist applications. Cloud hosting, security, and managed infrastructure may add recurring cost as well. These figures are relatively easy to estimate because they are visible and predictable.

Implementation is where scope matters most. A [focused deployment](/en/odoo-erp/) for finance, CRM, sales, purchasing, and basic inventory might require a project budget in the $20,000 to $60,000 range. A company with multiple legal entities, warehouses, manufacturing requirements, field teams, e-commerce channels, or complex reporting can reasonably invest $60,000 to $200,000 or more.

Those are planning ranges, not price lists. A 15-person distributor with clean data and disciplined processes may cost less to implement than a 50-person services business with inconsistent project billing and five disconnected systems. Headcount is only one indicator. Process complexity drives the work.

## What You Are Actually Paying For

A capable ERP partner is not paid simply to switch on modules. The work is to understand how orders move through the company, where approvals fail, which data finance trusts, and where staff compensate for weak systems with spreadsheets and memory.

### Process design and configuration

Standard configuration is often the best place to begin. It keeps maintenance manageable and enables faster upgrades. But standard does not mean careless. Your chart of accounts, approval flows, sales stages, warehouse rules, payment terms, and reporting structure need to reflect the way your company operates.

The cost rises when the business needs workflows that cannot be sensibly handled by configuration alone. [Custom development](/en/development/) can be valuable when it removes repetitive work, protects margin, or supports a genuine competitive advantage. It is poor value when it merely preserves an outdated habit.

### Data migration and cleanup

Migration is regularly underestimated because old data looks usable until someone tries to import it. Duplicate customers, inconsistent product codes, missing tax information, obsolete price lists, and incomplete opening balances all create risk.

You are not obligated to move every historical record. Many businesses migrate active customers, suppliers, products, open invoices, current inventory, and selected history, while retaining the legacy system in read-only form. This reduces cost and avoids carrying years of poor data into a new platform.

### Integrations and custom development

An ERP rarely operates alone. It may need to exchange data with an e-commerce platform, payment provider, shipping tool, payroll system, product information platform, customer portal, or business intelligence tool.

Some connections are standard and relatively straightforward. Others require custom APIs, middleware, error monitoring, and ongoing maintenance. Budgeting only for the initial connection is a mistake. An integration must continue to work when either platform changes.

### Training, testing, and change management

A well-configured ERP still fails if the team returns to Excel after two weeks. Staff need role-based training, practical test scenarios, and clear ownership of the new process. Managers need to reinforce the rules that make the data reliable.

Internal time is a real project cost. Your finance lead, operations manager, warehouse supervisor, and sales team will need to make decisions, test workflows, and validate data. If nobody has time to participate, the partner will make assumptions. Assumptions are expensive to correct later.

## The Cost Drivers That Matter Most

ERP budgets move for understandable reasons. Complexity is not always bad, but it must be recognized early.

A single company using one currency and a simple sales-to-invoice process is fundamentally different from a group with several entities, intercompany transactions, different tax rules, and consolidated reporting. Similarly, a business that ships from one location is easier to support than one managing multiple warehouses, serial tracking, replenishment rules, returns, and [third-party logistics providers](/en/solution-transport-logistics/).

Manufacturing can add another level of effort through bills of materials, routings, work centers, quality controls, subcontracting, and production planning. Project-based organizations may need accurate time capture, contract billing, expense allocation, and profitability reporting. Each requirement may be justified. Each requirement should also have a measurable business reason.

The condition of the current environment matters too. If you already use Odoo but have unreliable reporting, abandoned customizations, or unclear access rights, a recovery assessment may be the right first investment. Rebuilding blindly can cost more than diagnosing the problem properly.

## Why the Cheapest ERP Quote Can Cost More

A low quote can be legitimate when the scope is genuinely small. It becomes dangerous when it leaves critical work unnamed. Common omissions include data cleanup, test cycles, training, integrations, documentation, post-go-live support, and project management.

Ask what the quote assumes. How many workshops are included? Which workflows are configured? What data will be migrated? How many test cycles are planned? What happens if users identify a gap during acceptance testing? Who owns support after launch?

Also ask where customization is proposed and why. A good partner should be willing to challenge a request if standard functionality meets the business need. Agitech takes that view because unnecessary development creates cost now and maintenance later. The right answer is not always more code.

## Build a Budget Around Business Value

The strongest ERP business cases start with operational pain, not software features. Quantify the time spent rekeying orders, chasing stock information, reconciling invoices, correcting pricing errors, preparing reports, or resolving disputes caused by conflicting customer data.

Then identify what better control is worth. For one company, the return may come from fewer picking errors and better inventory planning. For another, it may come from faster invoicing, more accurate project margins, or the ability to close month-end without a week of manual reconciliation.

A phased approach often protects both budget and momentum. Launch the core workflows that create a dependable operational record first. Add advanced automation, customer portals, complex analytics, or specialist integrations once the team is working confidently in the foundation. This is not an excuse to postpone essential requirements. It is a way to avoid paying for theoretical needs before they are proven.

## A Practical Way to Control ERP Costs

Before requesting proposals, define the outcomes you need in plain language. For example: one customer record across sales and finance, live visibility of available stock, invoices created from confirmed orders, or profitability by project. These statements are more useful than a long list of features.

Give potential partners access to the realities of the business: sample documents, current reports, exceptions, and the spreadsheets people rely on. Be candid about what is broken. A proposal based on real workflows will be more accurate than one built from broad assumptions.

Set aside contingency for decisions that emerge during discovery. A reasonable contingency is not a blank check. It is a controlled allowance, approved against priorities, for work that genuinely improves the outcome.

The useful question is not whether ERP is cheap. It is whether the investment replaces avoidable labor, prevents costly errors, improves financial control, and gives management reliable information while the business grows. A clear scope, clean data, and honest advice do more to control ERP cost than an artificially low starting price ever will.
